1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,125
♪♪

2
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,375
♪♪

3
00:01:30,490 --> 00:01:32,686
♪♪

4
00:01:52,179 --> 00:01:53,623
Watch the floor, gentlemen.

5
00:01:53,647 --> 00:01:56,426
Keep those people
back, will you, John?

6
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,494
He was a law clerk?

7
00:01:58,518 --> 00:02:00,096
Terry Lasher.

8
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:01,898
I met him the other
day at the courthouse.

9
00:02:01,922 --> 00:02:04,567
I had no idea that's
who I was talking to.

10
00:02:04,591 --> 00:02:06,069
Makes sense, though...

11
00:02:06,093 --> 00:02:08,538
Two of the suspects are judges;
the other one's an attorney.

12
00:02:08,562 --> 00:02:10,106
Yeah, I guess he stumbled onto

13
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:11,674
whatever it is they were up to

14
00:02:11,698 --> 00:02:13,843
and didn't know
what to do about it.

15
00:02:13,867 --> 00:02:15,845
Should have just turned them in.

16
00:02:15,869 --> 00:02:17,347
Bad career move.

17
00:02:17,371 --> 00:02:20,049
Nobody likes a whistle
blower but the press.

18
00:02:20,073 --> 00:02:21,939
I wonder what tipped them off.

19
00:02:24,378 --> 00:02:25,855
I think I did.

20
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:27,290
You did?

21
00:02:27,314 --> 00:02:29,392
I must have shaken
them up so bad today,

22
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:31,394
they started following me.

23
00:02:31,418 --> 00:02:35,331
They recognized him, figured out
what was going on and shot him.

24
00:02:35,355 --> 00:02:38,668
Well, just found the slug.

25
00:02:38,692 --> 00:02:40,003
It's a .38 caliber.

26
00:02:40,027 --> 00:02:41,471
Lab will know quick enough

27
00:02:41,495 --> 00:02:45,274
whether it came from the same
gun that killed Howard Wright.

28
00:02:45,298 --> 00:02:47,744
Did you check up on Tom O'Hare?

29
00:02:47,768 --> 00:02:50,647
I sent a unit to his house
as soon as I got your call.

30
00:02:50,671 --> 00:02:53,683
He was there; he said
he had come home at 7:00

31
00:02:53,707 --> 00:02:55,752
and been there
ever since... <i>alone.</i>

32
00:02:55,776 --> 00:02:58,988
Oh. I don't suppose he
told you where he was

33
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:01,124
the night Howard
Wright was murdered?

34
00:03:01,148 --> 00:03:04,127
Working in his office, <i>alone.</i>

35
00:03:04,151 --> 00:03:05,662
So, what do we do now?

36
00:03:05,686 --> 00:03:07,730
I'm damned if I know, Conrad.

37
00:03:07,754 --> 00:03:09,699
I guess we just keep
doing what he said

38
00:03:09,723 --> 00:03:12,693
and look for the money.

39
00:03:17,631 --> 00:03:19,122
<i>Here's another one.</i>

40
00:03:21,068 --> 00:03:23,780
$370,000 was deposited

41
00:03:23,804 --> 00:03:25,415
into the Connor
Corporation account

42
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:27,784
on 8/28/90.

43
00:03:27,808 --> 00:03:30,820
That makes close to
$3 million, all coming into

44
00:03:30,844 --> 00:03:32,622
the company within
the last three years.

45
00:03:32,646 --> 00:03:34,724
But where's it coming from?

46
00:03:34,748 --> 00:03:36,793
It has to be money
they've gotten illegally.

47
00:03:36,817 --> 00:03:39,796
Maybe they're using the
Connor Corporation to launder it.

48
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:42,198
Well, we're talking about
judges and attorneys here,

49
00:03:42,222 --> 00:03:43,332
not drug dealers.

50
00:03:43,356 --> 00:03:44,667
I mean, what could they be into

51
00:03:44,691 --> 00:03:46,703
that involves that
kind of money?

52
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:49,138
- Anybody want more coffee?
- Yeah, I do. Thanks.

53
00:03:49,162 --> 00:03:51,631
Look at this.

54
00:03:54,534 --> 00:03:56,579
Sam Bergstrom.
Who's Sam Bergstrom?

55
00:03:56,603 --> 00:03:58,448
Connor Corporation wrote
him a lot of big checks.

56
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:00,483
Every time Tom O'Hare
and David Bennett got one,

57
00:04:00,507 --> 00:04:01,484
he got one, too.

58
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:04,153
Only his were for deposit
only into an account

59
00:04:04,177 --> 00:04:07,011
at the South Security
Bank of Savannah.

60
00:04:08,615 --> 00:04:11,278
How much gas
you got in your car?

61
00:04:17,157 --> 00:04:19,669
Superior Court
is now in session;

62
00:04:19,693 --> 00:04:22,686
the Honorable Judge
Diana Levin presiding.

63
00:04:23,830 --> 00:04:26,008
Ladies and
gentlemen of the jury,

64
00:04:26,032 --> 00:04:28,377
have you reached a verdict?

65
00:04:28,401 --> 00:04:30,580
Yes, Your Honor, we have.

66
00:04:30,604 --> 00:04:33,082
The jury finds for the plaintiff

67
00:04:33,106 --> 00:04:34,884
on all causes of action.

68
00:04:34,908 --> 00:04:37,086
Damages for lost
profits are found

69
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:39,255
in the amount of $2.2 million,

70
00:04:39,279 --> 00:04:43,059
for general damages in
the amount of $5 million,

71
00:04:43,083 --> 00:04:44,460
and for punitive damages

72
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:47,196
in the amount of $1.3 million.

73
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:48,231
Thank you.

74
00:04:48,255 --> 00:04:50,366
It has been a long, hard case.

75
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:52,668
We appreciate your
performing your civic duties.

76
00:04:52,692 --> 00:04:53,970
You are discharged.

77
00:04:53,994 --> 00:04:57,774
This court will stand
in recess until 2:00.

78
00:04:58,799 --> 00:05:00,791
I... I can't believe it!

79
00:05:04,171 --> 00:05:06,082
I still can't believe
it! We did it!

80
00:05:06,106 --> 00:05:07,116
We won the case.

81
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:08,184
You deserved to win, Jim.

82
00:05:08,208 --> 00:05:09,318
It's called justice.

83
00:05:09,342 --> 00:05:10,520
You think they'll appeal?

84
00:05:10,544 --> 00:05:11,754
They might, but I doubt it.

85
00:05:11,778 --> 00:05:14,657
$8 million for Maxwell
Toys is not that much money.

86
00:05:14,681 --> 00:05:16,926
May be cheaper for
them just to pay you off.

87
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:18,127
You know, about that letter...

88
00:05:18,151 --> 00:05:19,796
No, that doll was your idea.

89
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:22,498
You sent them a letter, and
they stole what was in it, right?

90
00:05:22,522 --> 00:05:23,699
Right, but...

91
00:05:23,723 --> 00:05:26,335
You are entitled
to the money, Jim.

92
00:05:26,359 --> 00:05:30,206
So there's no need to talk about
that letter now or ever again.

93
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:31,974
- Okay?
- Okay.

94
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:33,676
You take care.

95
00:05:39,739 --> 00:05:41,884
Hey, Mr. O'Hare.

96
00:05:41,908 --> 00:05:43,553
Congratulations.

97
00:05:43,577 --> 00:05:44,687
Thanks.

98
00:05:44,711 --> 00:05:46,022
Yeah.

99
00:05:46,046 --> 00:05:47,790
What's your usual contingency?

100
00:05:47,814 --> 00:05:49,025
One-third?

101
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:52,562
Well, boy, let me...
Hell, you just made...

102
00:05:52,586 --> 00:05:55,064
nearly $3 million in there.

103
00:05:55,088 --> 00:05:57,166
Is this what a
special prosecutor

104
00:05:57,190 --> 00:05:59,001
does all day, Mr. Matlock?

105
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:01,170
Hangs around the
courthouse, harassing people?

106
00:06:01,194 --> 00:06:03,239
Oh, no, no, no.

107
00:06:03,263 --> 00:06:04,473
I never harass people

108
00:06:04,497 --> 00:06:07,143
unless I'm thinking
of prosecuting them.

109
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:10,646
I told you before, I barely
knew Howard Wright.

110
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:13,149
Maybe that's why you killed him.

111
00:06:13,173 --> 00:06:16,619
Maybe you weren't sure of
how he would react when the

112
00:06:16,643 --> 00:06:18,354
police started questioning him

113
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:21,157
about that evidence
that he misplaced

114
00:06:21,181 --> 00:06:23,259
in the Jimmy Giles case.

115
00:06:23,283 --> 00:06:25,294
You're out of your mind.

116
00:06:25,318 --> 00:06:27,997
I know about the money
you've been getting

117
00:06:28,021 --> 00:06:31,500
from the Connor Corporation...
You and Judge Bennett.

118
00:06:31,524 --> 00:06:34,670
And when I find out why
you've been getting it,

119
00:06:34,694 --> 00:06:36,939
I may just come after you

120
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:39,175
for the murder of Terry Lasher.

121
00:06:39,199 --> 00:06:40,576
All right, that's it.

122
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:42,812
I've had it with cops
showing up on my doorstep

123
00:06:42,836 --> 00:06:44,280
every time somebody
dies in this town,

124
00:06:44,304 --> 00:06:45,581
and I've had it with you.

125
00:06:45,605 --> 00:06:48,885
If I hear you've repeated
this preposterous story

126
00:06:48,909 --> 00:06:52,021
to anyone, I'll file
a defamation suit

127
00:06:52,045 --> 00:06:53,656
that will leave you

128
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:55,725
financially crippled for life!

129
00:06:55,749 --> 00:06:57,741
Do you hear me?!

130
00:07:02,289 --> 00:07:05,123
I think everybody in
the parking lot heard you.

131
00:07:06,159 --> 00:07:08,151
Oh, yeah.

132
00:08:11,291 --> 00:08:13,269
Come on! What are you doing?

133
00:08:13,293 --> 00:08:16,539
What's the hurry?
Will you stop it, please?

134
00:08:16,563 --> 00:08:18,574
I'm too old for this.
Will you stop it?

135
00:08:18,598 --> 00:08:21,110
Stop it!

136
00:08:23,069 --> 00:08:24,970
Hey, wait a minute!

137
00:08:27,307 --> 00:08:29,919
Will you stop it? My God!

138
00:08:31,111 --> 00:08:33,456
Hey, sir?

139
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:38,294
Uh, is his bark
worse than his bite?

140
00:08:39,319 --> 00:08:41,697
Actually, his bite's pretty bad.

141
00:08:44,157 --> 00:08:45,935
Otis, will you stop that now?

142
00:08:45,959 --> 00:08:47,536
Get in the house.

143
00:08:48,561 --> 00:08:50,706
Oh. Oh.

144
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:52,308
Damn dog.

145
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,513
Do you take him on... a
walk every night at this time?

146
00:08:57,537 --> 00:09:01,117
Well, it's good to keep
a dog on schedule.

147
00:09:01,141 --> 00:09:02,651
Makes things more predictable.

148
00:09:02,675 --> 00:09:05,187
7:53 p.m. every night,

149
00:09:05,211 --> 00:09:08,391
Bethany's lawn, come
rain or come shine.

150
00:09:08,415 --> 00:09:10,126
Hey.

151
00:09:10,150 --> 00:09:12,728
Um, last Wednesday night,

152
00:09:12,752 --> 00:09:15,631
when you passed the
Wrights' house over there,

153
00:09:15,655 --> 00:09:20,389
did you see or hear
anything out of the ordinary?

154
00:09:21,828 --> 00:09:23,939
Last Wednesday night...

155
00:09:23,963 --> 00:09:26,592
The night Howard
Wright was killed.

156
00:09:27,834 --> 00:09:29,779
No.

157
00:09:29,803 --> 00:09:31,113
No, I-I-I'm sorry.

158
00:09:31,137 --> 00:09:32,815
You did see something.

159
00:09:32,839 --> 00:09:36,085
Wait a minute,
uh... Who are you?

160
00:09:36,109 --> 00:09:38,421
My name's Ben
Matlock; I'm a lawyer.

161
00:09:38,445 --> 00:09:41,924
Well, I don't want
to get involved.

162
00:09:41,948 --> 00:09:43,859
But if you saw something,

163
00:09:43,883 --> 00:09:45,361
you have to get involved.

164
00:09:45,385 --> 00:09:46,829
It's your duty.

165
00:09:46,853 --> 00:09:50,433
I just don't want some
killer coming after me next.

166
00:09:50,457 --> 00:09:52,868
That-That-That won't happen.

167
00:09:52,892 --> 00:09:55,337
That won't happen.
I... I promise you.

168
00:09:55,361 --> 00:09:57,023
Okay.

169
00:10:09,576 --> 00:10:11,253
Mr. O'Hare?

170
00:10:11,277 --> 00:10:13,722
Mr. O'Hare, you're under arrest

171
00:10:13,746 --> 00:10:15,458
for the murder of Howard Wright.

172
00:10:15,482 --> 00:10:17,159
What?

173
00:10:17,183 --> 00:10:18,360
We have an eyewitness

174
00:10:18,384 --> 00:10:20,029
who saw you leaving
the scene of the crime.

175
00:10:20,053 --> 00:10:21,263
All right. That's it, Matlock.

176
00:10:21,287 --> 00:10:22,631
You were warned,
and you're gonna pay.

177
00:10:22,655 --> 00:10:24,500
- Just come with us, Mr. O'Hare.
- False arrest

178
00:10:24,524 --> 00:10:26,168
and defamation of character...
That's just for starters.

179
00:10:26,192 --> 00:10:27,736
You can't have enough
malpractice insurance

180
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:29,505
to cover the charges I'm
gonna bring against you.

181
00:10:29,529 --> 00:10:31,240
- Your career is over, Mr. Matlock!
- You have the right

182
00:10:31,264 --> 00:10:33,542
- to remain silent.
- You'll never make another cent!

183
00:10:33,566 --> 00:10:34,567
Any statement... any
statement you make...

184
00:10:34,591 --> 00:10:35,010
You're gonna die!

185
00:10:35,034 --> 00:10:37,213
- May and will be held against...
- There's a bullet for you!

186
00:10:37,237 --> 00:10:38,380
You hear me?!

187
00:10:38,404 --> 00:10:40,282
Your career is
over, Mr. Matlock!

188
00:10:43,009 --> 00:10:45,001
He'll get over it.

189
00:11:12,005 --> 00:11:15,151
It's gonna take a while to
get used to sitting over here.

190
00:11:15,175 --> 00:11:16,404
Mm-hmm, it is.

191
00:11:17,610 --> 00:11:20,289
Who's Mr. O'Hare
got defending him?

192
00:11:20,313 --> 00:11:22,057
Chester Gaddis.

193
00:11:22,081 --> 00:11:23,926
Member of the
bar in five states,

194
00:11:23,950 --> 00:11:26,283
hasn't lost a
murder one case yet.

195
00:11:31,925 --> 00:11:34,904
There's something I want
you to find out about him.

196
00:11:34,928 --> 00:11:36,005
How much he charges?

197
00:11:36,029 --> 00:11:37,006
- Yeah.
- Hmm.

198
00:11:37,030 --> 00:11:40,576
Moving right along
to the matter of bail.

199
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:43,832
Does the, uh, prosecution
have any recommendations?

200
00:11:45,572 --> 00:11:47,583
Ah, yes, sir.

201
00:11:47,607 --> 00:11:51,153
Uh, not only has the
defendant been charged

202
00:11:51,177 --> 00:11:54,323
with the heinous crime
of premeditated murder,

203
00:11:54,347 --> 00:11:56,392
but he is considerably wealthy

204
00:11:56,416 --> 00:12:00,029
and has no family to
bind him to the community.

205
00:12:00,053 --> 00:12:02,298
The defense...
uh, prosecution...

206
00:12:02,322 --> 00:12:07,603
Therefore recommends
bail be set at $1 million.

207
00:12:07,627 --> 00:12:09,619
Defense?

208
00:12:11,864 --> 00:12:15,644
Your Honor, uh, my
client has been accused

209
00:12:15,668 --> 00:12:18,013
of a heinous crime, yes,

210
00:12:18,037 --> 00:12:19,415
but proven guilty, no.

211
00:12:19,439 --> 00:12:22,084
And not only has he
been an officer of the court

212
00:12:22,108 --> 00:12:25,221
for 15 years, but
his contributions

213
00:12:25,245 --> 00:12:28,190
to various
philanthropic activities

214
00:12:28,214 --> 00:12:31,560
have made him a pillar
of the very community

215
00:12:31,584 --> 00:12:34,196
the state would now have
you believe he wishes to flee.

216
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:37,533
I see no risk here whatever.

217
00:12:37,557 --> 00:12:40,636
I recommend bail
not exceed $100,000.

218
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:43,357
I tend to agree, Mr. Gaddis.

219
00:12:44,397 --> 00:12:46,875
Bail is set at $100,000.

220
00:12:46,899 --> 00:12:48,077
Thank you, Your Honor.

221
00:12:48,101 --> 00:12:50,579
He's good.

222
00:12:50,603 --> 00:12:52,834
He dresses funny.

223
00:13:05,718 --> 00:13:07,529
Hi.

224
00:13:07,553 --> 00:13:09,765
I'm looking for Sam Bergstrom.

225
00:13:09,789 --> 00:13:11,100
What can I do for you?

226
00:13:11,124 --> 00:13:12,201
You're Sam Bergstrom?

227
00:13:12,225 --> 00:13:13,569
The one that has an account

228
00:13:13,593 --> 00:13:15,971
over at the South Security Bank?

229
00:13:15,995 --> 00:13:17,640
Well, life is too short

230
00:13:17,664 --> 00:13:20,442
to be writing "Samantha"
out all the time.

231
00:13:20,466 --> 00:13:22,044
Uh, who are you?

232
00:13:22,068 --> 00:13:24,313
Uh, I'm Russell Yeager, ma'am.

233
00:13:24,337 --> 00:13:25,681
I'm from the IRS.

234
00:13:25,705 --> 00:13:27,616
May I come in?

235
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,919
Invite somebody from the biggest
group of crooks in the country

236
00:13:30,943 --> 00:13:32,343
into my home?

237
00:13:33,846 --> 00:13:36,225
Not on your life.

238
00:13:36,249 --> 00:13:39,686
Well, I need to ask you
a few questions, ma'am.

239
00:13:43,189 --> 00:13:45,701
Go ahead.

240
00:13:45,725 --> 00:13:49,371
We know some rather large
checks from the Connor Corporation

241
00:13:49,395 --> 00:13:52,341
up in Atlanta have been
deposited in your account

242
00:13:52,365 --> 00:13:53,942
over the last couple of years.

243
00:13:53,966 --> 00:13:55,411
Are you an employee of theirs?

244
00:13:55,435 --> 00:13:56,945
No.

245
00:13:56,969 --> 00:13:58,080
Well, well, then why

246
00:13:58,104 --> 00:14:00,115
are they paying you
all that money, ma'am?

247
00:14:00,139 --> 00:14:02,484
Why do you want to know?

248
00:14:02,508 --> 00:14:03,919
Well, there have been
some irregularities

249
00:14:03,943 --> 00:14:05,988
at the Connor Corporation.

250
00:14:06,012 --> 00:14:08,057
We're auditing all
their expenditures.

251
00:14:08,081 --> 00:14:10,192
Uh, y-you're not auditing me?

252
00:14:10,216 --> 00:14:11,694
You? No, no.

253
00:14:11,718 --> 00:14:14,196
Good.

254
00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:16,198
That money belongs to my niece.

255
00:14:16,222 --> 00:14:17,199
Your niece?

256
00:14:17,223 --> 00:14:19,935
Yes. Her accountant suggested
that she keep it in my name

257
00:14:19,959 --> 00:14:22,237
for-for tax purposes.

258
00:14:22,261 --> 00:14:24,506
I don't understand
all the ins and outs,

259
00:14:24,530 --> 00:14:26,575
but I'm sure it's
quite legitimate...

260
00:14:26,599 --> 00:14:28,444
In view of her job and all.

261
00:14:28,468 --> 00:14:29,878
Which is what?

262
00:14:29,902 --> 00:14:31,680
She's a judge.

263
00:14:31,704 --> 00:14:34,883
Judge Diana Levin.

264
00:14:34,907 --> 00:14:38,120
Got your message. What's... up?

265
00:14:39,846 --> 00:14:42,424
I-I'm a bad man, Your Honor.

266
00:14:42,448 --> 00:14:45,861
Uh, I'm the one who
slipped your clerk that note.

267
00:14:45,885 --> 00:14:47,863
We need to talk.

268
00:14:47,887 --> 00:14:48,498
Sit down here.

269
00:14:48,522 --> 00:14:49,665
Yes.

270
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:54,803
Uh, an associate of
mine spoke to your aunt

271
00:14:54,827 --> 00:14:58,006
down in Savannah today,
and you've been getting payoffs

272
00:14:58,030 --> 00:15:00,642
from the Connor Corporation
just like Judge Bennett

273
00:15:00,666 --> 00:15:03,145
- here and Tom O'Hare.
- Those aren't

274
00:15:03,169 --> 00:15:04,980
payoffs, those are

275
00:15:05,004 --> 00:15:06,281
- consulting fees.
- Consulting fees.

276
00:15:06,305 --> 00:15:08,050
I don't think so.

277
00:15:08,074 --> 00:15:12,221
I think the three of you are
using the Connor Corporation

278
00:15:12,245 --> 00:15:15,724
to hold and distribute money
that you've come by illegally.

279
00:15:15,748 --> 00:15:17,526
You are dead wrong.

280
00:15:17,550 --> 00:15:20,996
I know your scheme has
something to do with Maxwell Toys.

281
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:22,531
And when the truth comes out

282
00:15:22,555 --> 00:15:25,100
in court, and it will,

283
00:15:25,124 --> 00:15:28,103
why, you'll go down right
along with Mr. O'Hare.

284
00:15:28,127 --> 00:15:31,206
So, unless prison is
something you're just dying for,

285
00:15:31,230 --> 00:15:34,009
if I were you, I'd tell me
what's going on right now.

286
00:15:34,033 --> 00:15:37,479
You called this meeting
to offer us a deal?

287
00:15:37,503 --> 00:15:38,503
One time only.

288
00:15:39,906 --> 00:15:41,898
Oh, and-and to give you this.

289
00:15:42,942 --> 00:15:44,920
- A subpoena.
- Uh-huh.

290
00:15:44,944 --> 00:15:48,524
You are wasting
your time and mine.

291
00:15:48,548 --> 00:15:51,780
Well-Well, it's, uh... uh...

292
00:15:53,019 --> 00:15:54,830
I don't have anything
more to say to you, either.

293
00:15:54,854 --> 00:15:56,832
Okay.

294
00:15:56,856 --> 00:16:01,470
I guess I'll... see
you both in court.

295
00:16:01,494 --> 00:16:03,972
Enjoy.

296
00:16:25,151 --> 00:16:26,462
- Thank you.
- Yes.

297
00:16:26,486 --> 00:16:27,629
Thank you for coming.

298
00:16:27,653 --> 00:16:29,998
To be honest, I'm
not sure why I'm here.

299
00:16:30,022 --> 00:16:31,354
Please.

300
00:16:34,327 --> 00:16:35,471
Some wine?

301
00:16:35,495 --> 00:16:37,157
No, thank you.

302
00:16:41,734 --> 00:16:43,225
Michelle.

303
00:16:49,041 --> 00:16:51,320
Michelle, I, uh...

304
00:16:51,344 --> 00:16:54,456
I swear to you, I didn't
have anything to do

305
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:56,358
with Howard Wright's murder.

306
00:16:56,382 --> 00:16:58,627
I didn't do it, and I
don't know who did.

307
00:16:58,651 --> 00:17:01,396
You've got to tell Ben
Matlock to back off

308
00:17:01,420 --> 00:17:03,799
before he ruins me.

309
00:17:03,823 --> 00:17:06,735
That's why you asked
me to come here?

310
00:17:06,759 --> 00:17:09,071
I meant something
to you once, Michelle.

311
00:17:09,095 --> 00:17:11,273
I don't deserve
this, and you know it.

312
00:17:11,297 --> 00:17:13,375
Just tell him the truth...
Tell him it wasn't me.

313
00:17:13,399 --> 00:17:16,211
I can't believe you'd
try to use me like this.

314
00:17:16,235 --> 00:17:17,646
What choice do I have?

315
00:17:17,670 --> 00:17:19,014
My back's against the wall.

316
00:17:19,038 --> 00:17:20,249
I can't do anything else.

317
00:17:20,273 --> 00:17:21,550
You can tell us the truth.

318
00:17:21,574 --> 00:17:23,151
I am, I swear.

319
00:17:23,175 --> 00:17:25,320
The money you get from
the Connor Corporation...

320
00:17:25,344 --> 00:17:27,745
Where does it come from?

321
00:17:34,120 --> 00:17:35,497
I'm sorry.

322
00:17:35,521 --> 00:17:36,965
If you want us to help you,

323
00:17:36,989 --> 00:17:39,288
you're going to have
to tell us the truth.

324
00:17:41,994 --> 00:17:44,973
You, uh...

325
00:17:44,997 --> 00:17:49,011
You've turned into one
tough lady, Michelle.

326
00:17:49,035 --> 00:17:51,947
I'm not sure it becomes you.

327
00:17:51,971 --> 00:17:54,583
David, you're in a
great deal of trouble.

328
00:17:54,607 --> 00:17:57,475
I'd like to help you.

329
00:18:04,216 --> 00:18:06,276
It's up to you.

330
00:18:15,061 --> 00:18:16,371
<i>Well, what did you</i>

331
00:18:16,395 --> 00:18:19,174
see when you walked your dog

332
00:18:19,198 --> 00:18:22,444
past Howard Wright's house
that night, Mr. Spaulding?

333
00:18:22,468 --> 00:18:25,247
I saw a man rushing
out of the garage,

334
00:18:25,271 --> 00:18:28,083
jump into his car and
go tearing out of there.

335
00:18:28,107 --> 00:18:29,651
Uh-huh.

336
00:18:29,675 --> 00:18:33,121
And is the man you saw that
night in this courtroom today?

337
00:18:33,145 --> 00:18:34,656
Oh, yeah.

338
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:36,491
He's sitting right over there.

339
00:18:36,515 --> 00:18:40,329
Let the record show that
the witness has pointed

340
00:18:40,353 --> 00:18:42,864
to the defendant, Tom O'Hare.

341
00:18:42,888 --> 00:18:45,233
Do you remember what
time it was you saw him?

342
00:18:45,257 --> 00:18:47,035
- Around 8:00.
- You sure?

343
00:18:47,059 --> 00:18:48,403
Positive.

344
00:18:48,427 --> 00:18:49,972
It's always around that time

345
00:18:49,996 --> 00:18:52,407
that Otis and I pass
the Wrights' house.

346
00:18:52,431 --> 00:18:54,710
It's good to keep a dog
on schedule, you know.

347
00:18:54,734 --> 00:18:57,279
Yeah, you told me
that, yeah, yeah.

348
00:18:57,303 --> 00:19:01,016
So you're sure the man you
saw leaving the scene of the crime

349
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:05,153
at, uh, about 8:00... which
the-the coroner has established

350
00:19:05,177 --> 00:19:09,324
to be the approximate time
of the murder... y-you're sure

351
00:19:09,348 --> 00:19:12,160
that man was the
defendant, Tom O'Hare?

352
00:19:12,184 --> 00:19:14,062
Positive.

353
00:19:14,086 --> 00:19:15,987
Thank you.

354
00:19:17,123 --> 00:19:19,024
Cross-examine?

355
00:19:28,300 --> 00:19:30,269
Mr. Spaulding...

356
00:19:32,471 --> 00:19:38,854
Where were you and Otis
the night you saw this person

357
00:19:38,878 --> 00:19:40,422
leaving the garage?

358
00:19:40,446 --> 00:19:42,457
At the end of the driveway.

359
00:19:42,481 --> 00:19:45,527
And could you
see his face clearly

360
00:19:45,551 --> 00:19:47,529
after he entered the car?

361
00:19:47,553 --> 00:19:48,919
No.

362
00:19:50,322 --> 00:19:54,169
So the only time you saw him

363
00:19:54,193 --> 00:19:56,838
was after he exited the garage

364
00:19:56,862 --> 00:19:59,274
and just before he entered
the car, is that correct?

365
00:19:59,298 --> 00:20:02,277
- Right.
- Oh.

366
00:20:02,301 --> 00:20:06,481
And approximately
what distance was it

367
00:20:06,505 --> 00:20:08,303
from the garage to the car?

368
00:20:09,975 --> 00:20:12,721
Oh, maybe... ten feet.

369
00:20:12,745 --> 00:20:15,490
And approximately
how long did it take him

370
00:20:15,514 --> 00:20:16,925
to cover that distance?

371
00:20:16,949 --> 00:20:19,227
Six, seven seconds.

372
00:20:19,251 --> 00:20:20,395
Six seconds?

373
00:20:20,419 --> 00:20:22,364
To cover ten feet?

374
00:20:22,388 --> 00:20:24,499
I thought you said
he was in a hurry.

375
00:20:24,523 --> 00:20:30,906
Well, uh... Maybe
three... four... seconds.

376
00:20:30,930 --> 00:20:33,308
Three seconds, tops. I timed it.

377
00:20:33,332 --> 00:20:36,378
I also measured the distance
from the end of the driveway

378
00:20:36,402 --> 00:20:38,714
to the garage.

379
00:20:38,738 --> 00:20:40,515
It's 38 yards.

380
00:20:40,539 --> 00:20:42,084
Does that sound
about right to you?

381
00:20:42,108 --> 00:20:43,552
I guess so.

382
00:20:43,576 --> 00:20:45,721
Huh.

383
00:20:45,745 --> 00:20:50,992
Can you guess how many
lights illuminated the garage door?

384
00:20:51,016 --> 00:20:52,728
Two, three.

385
00:20:52,752 --> 00:20:53,845
One.

386
00:20:55,221 --> 00:20:56,832
How's your eyesight,
Mr. Spaulding?

387
00:20:56,856 --> 00:20:57,999
Do you wear glasses?

388
00:20:58,023 --> 00:20:59,000
Only for reading.

389
00:20:59,024 --> 00:21:02,204
So your vision
isn't exactly perfect?

390
00:21:02,228 --> 00:21:04,272
No, it's not perfect,
but I don't find...

391
00:21:04,296 --> 00:21:06,141
Did you happen to
catch the license plate

392
00:21:06,165 --> 00:21:07,876
on the car that
night, Mr. Spaulding?

393
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:09,511
No.

394
00:21:09,535 --> 00:21:11,279
Did you catch the
make of the car?

395
00:21:11,303 --> 00:21:13,101
No.

396
00:21:14,707 --> 00:21:16,369
How about the color?

397
00:21:17,376 --> 00:21:21,356
Color... Uh...

398
00:21:21,380 --> 00:21:23,645
beige, yellow,
something like that.

399
00:21:25,317 --> 00:21:28,776
How about the man's
clothes? What color were they?

400
00:21:30,022 --> 00:21:32,389
Uh, uh, d-dark.

401
00:21:34,126 --> 00:21:35,822
Dark?

402
00:21:36,829 --> 00:21:40,976
Mr. Spaulding, I don't doubt
you saw someone that night,

403
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:45,013
but whoever it was, you
saw him from a distance

404
00:21:45,037 --> 00:21:47,449
of 38 yards,

405
00:21:47,473 --> 00:21:51,787
by the light of a
single 40-watt bulb,

406
00:21:51,811 --> 00:21:55,323
for a grand total of
maybe three seconds.

407
00:21:55,347 --> 00:21:57,425
You can't remember
what his car looked like.

408
00:21:57,449 --> 00:21:59,661
You can't remember
what his clothes were like.

409
00:21:59,685 --> 00:22:02,330
Mr. Spaulding...

410
00:22:02,354 --> 00:22:06,052
you can't say for sure that
was my client, can you?

411
00:22:13,199 --> 00:22:16,378
No, I guess I can't.

412
00:22:16,402 --> 00:22:18,132
Thank you.

413
00:22:26,378 --> 00:22:27,622
I hate him.

414
00:22:27,646 --> 00:22:29,090
I don't.

415
00:22:29,114 --> 00:22:30,826
He reminds me of you.

416
00:22:30,850 --> 00:22:34,029
Michelle, he's sneaky,
conniving, deceitful.

417
00:22:34,053 --> 00:22:35,964
That's the cruelest thing
you've ever said to me.

418
00:22:35,988 --> 00:22:37,933
Oh, Ben, I just meant
he's good, that's all.

419
00:22:37,957 --> 00:22:39,701
I mean, you got
to give him that.

420
00:22:39,725 --> 00:22:40,818
Like hell, I do.

421
00:22:43,762 --> 00:22:45,173
He's not good.

422
00:22:45,197 --> 00:22:46,575
Okay.

423
00:22:46,599 --> 00:22:48,376
He's probably the best.

424
00:22:48,400 --> 00:22:50,494
Beating the devil out of us.

425
00:22:53,606 --> 00:22:55,837
I'm ready to cut a deal.

426
00:23:06,452 --> 00:23:09,564
<i>You've known the
defendant</i> for how long?

427
00:23:09,588 --> 00:23:11,166
About ten years.

428
00:23:11,190 --> 00:23:12,534
Friends?

429
00:23:12,558 --> 00:23:14,703
Not close friends.

430
00:23:14,727 --> 00:23:18,340
More like, uh...
business associates.

431
00:23:18,364 --> 00:23:21,425
What kind of business,
Judge Bennett?

432
00:23:22,935 --> 00:23:25,580
A couple of years ago,

433
00:23:25,604 --> 00:23:28,149
shortly after I was
appointed presiding judge,

434
00:23:28,173 --> 00:23:32,621
Tom O'Hare came to
me with a proposition.

435
00:23:32,645 --> 00:23:34,022
He said if I'd assign myself

436
00:23:34,046 --> 00:23:37,692
to a multimillion-dollar
lawsuit that he'd just filed and

437
00:23:37,716 --> 00:23:40,095
use all my powers
as presiding judge

438
00:23:40,119 --> 00:23:43,749
to see to it that the jury
ruled in his client's favor...

439
00:23:45,858 --> 00:23:50,605
he would split his one-third
of the judgment with me.

440
00:23:50,629 --> 00:23:53,341
So that's what we did.

441
00:23:53,365 --> 00:23:55,810
Did it work?

442
00:23:55,834 --> 00:23:59,314
So well that we did it
again... Many times.

443
00:23:59,338 --> 00:24:01,249
D-D-Do you mean to tell me

444
00:24:01,273 --> 00:24:04,886
that a judge can
actually fix a trial?

445
00:24:04,910 --> 00:24:06,721
Well, it's a, uh, obviously,

446
00:24:06,745 --> 00:24:09,491
very subtle thing, but... yes.

447
00:24:09,515 --> 00:24:12,494
Weren't you afraid that
somebody would, you know,

448
00:24:12,518 --> 00:24:14,763
catch on to your scheme?

449
00:24:14,787 --> 00:24:16,131
Very.

450
00:24:16,155 --> 00:24:18,900
That's why we eventually
got a second judge involved.

451
00:24:18,924 --> 00:24:21,336
Judge Diana Levin.

452
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:23,939
Yes.

453
00:24:23,963 --> 00:24:25,774
Instead of assigning
all these cases to myself,

454
00:24:25,798 --> 00:24:27,876
I was able to
assign some to her.

455
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:29,811
That way, we could
avert suspicion.

456
00:24:32,705 --> 00:24:33,748
Go ahead.

457
00:24:33,772 --> 00:24:35,450
She had a friend
named Glenn Connor

458
00:24:35,474 --> 00:24:38,687
who owned a holding company,
the Connor Corporation.

459
00:24:38,711 --> 00:24:39,788
Mm-hmm.

460
00:24:39,812 --> 00:24:40,956
Uh...

461
00:24:40,980 --> 00:24:45,393
I'm showing you a
ledger which was seized

462
00:24:45,417 --> 00:24:49,831
by a search warrant from
the Connor Corporation offices,

463
00:24:49,855 --> 00:24:53,068
and which, by
stipulation with counsel,

464
00:24:53,092 --> 00:24:56,471
will be marked
"People's Exhibit 37"

465
00:24:56,495 --> 00:24:58,573
and entered into evidence.

466
00:24:58,597 --> 00:25:00,759
Now, how was the
money laundered?

467
00:25:02,735 --> 00:25:05,146
After winning a judgment,
Tom would instruct his client

468
00:25:05,170 --> 00:25:08,483
to pay his usual one-third
contingency fee in the form of

469
00:25:08,507 --> 00:25:12,520
a cashier's check made
payable to a bank in the Bahamas.

470
00:25:12,544 --> 00:25:18,693
Like these three checks here
for $125,000 to each of you?

471
00:25:18,717 --> 00:25:19,961
Yes.

472
00:25:19,985 --> 00:25:22,764
And, uh, that was, uh,

473
00:25:22,788 --> 00:25:27,102
ostensibly for, uh...
uh, consulting work?

474
00:25:27,126 --> 00:25:28,603
That's correct.

475
00:25:28,627 --> 00:25:31,406
But you never did any
consulting work for them, did you?

476
00:25:31,430 --> 00:25:32,430
No.

477
00:25:34,266 --> 00:25:36,945
Now, this-this check here...

478
00:25:36,969 --> 00:25:40,515
Right here for $75,000,
made out to Howard Wright...

479
00:25:40,539 --> 00:25:41,883
What was that for?

480
00:25:41,907 --> 00:25:45,053
That was his payment for
losing a vital piece of evidence

481
00:25:45,077 --> 00:25:47,722
in a case he was prosecuting
against Jimmy Giles,

482
00:25:47,746 --> 00:25:50,125
the man accused of
breaking into Maxwell Toys.

483
00:25:50,149 --> 00:25:54,329
And who directed the Connor
Corporation to make the payment?

484
00:25:54,353 --> 00:25:56,231
Tom O'Hare.

485
00:25:56,255 --> 00:25:58,266
He was protecting Giles,

486
00:25:58,290 --> 00:26:00,201
whom he'd hired to
plant evidence for him.

487
00:26:00,225 --> 00:26:01,603
Oh, how do you know?

488
00:26:01,627 --> 00:26:03,204
He told me.

489
00:26:03,228 --> 00:26:04,706
Did he tell you anything else?

490
00:26:04,730 --> 00:26:06,307
Yes, he did.

491
00:26:06,331 --> 00:26:08,410
He told me that
he was afraid that

492
00:26:08,434 --> 00:26:10,078
Howard would break
down and confess

493
00:26:10,102 --> 00:26:11,880
if he was confronted
by the police.

494
00:26:11,904 --> 00:26:14,582
Oh.

495
00:26:14,606 --> 00:26:17,385
Oh, and if that had happened,

496
00:26:17,409 --> 00:26:19,421
what would've
happened to Mr. O'Hare?

497
00:26:19,445 --> 00:26:21,089
He would've been disbarred

498
00:26:21,113 --> 00:26:23,158
and probably gone to prison.

499
00:26:23,182 --> 00:26:24,159
Uh-huh.

500
00:26:24,183 --> 00:26:26,861
But that never happened, did it?

501
00:26:26,885 --> 00:26:29,597
Instead, Howard
Wright was murdered.

502
00:26:29,621 --> 00:26:31,613
Nothing further.

503
00:26:34,293 --> 00:26:37,286
Does defense wish to cross?

504
00:26:39,798 --> 00:26:44,012
I've been sitting here
quietly, Judge Bennett,

505
00:26:44,036 --> 00:26:47,048
because I've been waiting.

506
00:26:47,072 --> 00:26:49,064
For proof.

507
00:26:52,544 --> 00:26:55,523
And the problem is...

508
00:26:55,547 --> 00:26:58,793
that you don't have any.

509
00:26:58,817 --> 00:27:02,964
I-I mean, you can't prove
that my client was involved

510
00:27:02,988 --> 00:27:06,034
in this ingenious plan
of yours, can you?

511
00:27:06,058 --> 00:27:07,068
Of course I can.

512
00:27:07,092 --> 00:27:09,537
Just look at that ledger.

513
00:27:09,561 --> 00:27:10,839
Oh.

514
00:27:10,863 --> 00:27:13,875
Well, this doesn't
prove that my client,

515
00:27:13,899 --> 00:27:15,310
uh, never did any
consulting work

516
00:27:15,334 --> 00:27:16,644
for the Connor
Corporation, does it?

517
00:27:16,668 --> 00:27:18,012
Well, I know I didn't.

518
00:27:19,104 --> 00:27:20,982
I don't give a damn

519
00:27:21,006 --> 00:27:23,618
if you took money
under false pretenses,

520
00:27:23,642 --> 00:27:24,953
Judge Bennett.

521
00:27:24,977 --> 00:27:28,423
The question is: Can you prove

522
00:27:28,447 --> 00:27:31,726
that my client did?

523
00:27:31,750 --> 00:27:34,729
Well, uh... Can you prove

524
00:27:34,753 --> 00:27:37,098
that he paid Howard Wright

525
00:27:37,122 --> 00:27:39,934
to deliberately lose
a piece of evidence?

526
00:27:39,958 --> 00:27:42,370
No, but he... Oh,
uh, can you prove

527
00:27:42,394 --> 00:27:44,873
that he told you
that he was afraid

528
00:27:44,897 --> 00:27:46,774
Howard Wright was
gonna get him disbarred?

529
00:27:46,798 --> 00:27:50,245
No, but... No, but!

530
00:27:50,269 --> 00:27:53,148
You cannot... prove

531
00:27:53,172 --> 00:27:57,519
a single word of your
testimony against my client.

532
00:27:57,543 --> 00:27:59,354
You certainly haven't
offered any evidence

533
00:27:59,378 --> 00:28:01,890
that would involve him
in Howard Wright's death.

534
00:28:01,914 --> 00:28:05,627
Nor can you offer such
evidence, can you...

535
00:28:05,651 --> 00:28:07,762
Judge Bennett?

536
00:28:07,786 --> 00:28:08,763
It's what happened!

537
00:28:08,787 --> 00:28:11,499
Just because I can't prove
it doesn't mean it isn't true!

538
00:28:11,523 --> 00:28:13,801
We're just supposed
to take your word for it?

539
00:28:13,825 --> 00:28:15,937
Yes.

540
00:28:15,961 --> 00:28:17,953
I think not.

541
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:23,032
No further questions.

542
00:28:24,536 --> 00:28:25,947
Witness may step down.

543
00:28:25,971 --> 00:28:27,448
Ben? Ben?

544
00:28:27,472 --> 00:28:28,449
Hmm?

545
00:28:28,473 --> 00:28:31,671
Bob Brooks just got
this from the police lab.

546
00:28:33,679 --> 00:28:34,656
Oh.

547
00:28:34,680 --> 00:28:37,125
This is inexcusable!

548
00:28:37,149 --> 00:28:39,294
Mr. Matlock has had
the police department

549
00:28:39,318 --> 00:28:41,496
at his full disposal
since day one.

550
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:44,032
For them to produce
evidence on his behalf

551
00:28:44,056 --> 00:28:48,036
this late in the
day... it's outrageous!

552
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:50,805
Mr. Gaddis is absolutely
correct, Your Honor.

553
00:28:50,829 --> 00:28:52,907
The police lab took way too long

554
00:28:52,931 --> 00:28:54,709
to complete this analysis.

555
00:28:54,733 --> 00:28:59,080
I wish we could've gotten
it to him much earlier.

556
00:28:59,104 --> 00:29:02,717
Well, I just resent being
thrown a curve this...

557
00:29:02,741 --> 00:29:04,118
halfway through a trial.

558
00:29:04,142 --> 00:29:05,119
Yeah.

559
00:29:05,143 --> 00:29:07,622
You had full
disclosure, Mr. Gaddis.

560
00:29:07,646 --> 00:29:09,991
And you knew they
were running tests.

561
00:29:10,015 --> 00:29:11,426
A half-day recess

562
00:29:11,450 --> 00:29:13,061
should give you ample time

563
00:29:13,085 --> 00:29:15,630
to work the results
into your defense.

564
00:29:15,654 --> 00:29:18,499
Now, let's get back in there.

565
00:29:18,523 --> 00:29:21,202
We'll put your witness
on the stand, Mr. Matlock,

566
00:29:21,226 --> 00:29:23,218
and then recess till tomorrow.

567
00:29:25,731 --> 00:29:28,176
I don't like this one bit.

568
00:29:28,200 --> 00:29:29,532
I don't blame you.

569
00:29:31,536 --> 00:29:33,971
Uh... Lieutenant Brooks...

570
00:29:36,008 --> 00:29:39,153
When the police arrived
at Howard Wright's house

571
00:29:39,177 --> 00:29:40,588
the night of the murder,

572
00:29:40,612 --> 00:29:43,825
they found a fresh
puddle of transmission fluid

573
00:29:43,849 --> 00:29:45,426
outside his garage.
Is that correct?

574
00:29:45,450 --> 00:29:46,894
Yes, we did.

575
00:29:46,918 --> 00:29:48,596
Where'd it come from?

576
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:52,200
A comparative analysis performed
after the defendant was arrested

577
00:29:52,224 --> 00:29:54,435
shows it came from his car.

578
00:29:54,459 --> 00:29:55,970
Meaning the defendant's car

579
00:29:55,994 --> 00:29:59,173
was parked outside
his garage that night?

580
00:29:59,197 --> 00:30:00,275
Yes.

581
00:30:00,299 --> 00:30:01,876
Doesn't necessarily
mean the defendant

582
00:30:01,900 --> 00:30:03,645
was in the garage,
though, does it?

583
00:30:03,669 --> 00:30:06,481
No, but we also found a varnish

584
00:30:06,505 --> 00:30:09,584
on the floor of Howard
Wright's workshop that night.

585
00:30:09,608 --> 00:30:11,886
Uh, apparently, it had
spilled during the shooting.

586
00:30:11,910 --> 00:30:14,789
Forensics has isolated traces

587
00:30:14,813 --> 00:30:17,191
of that exact same
varnish on the fibers

588
00:30:17,215 --> 00:30:19,994
of the floor mat on the driver's
side of the defendant's car,

589
00:30:20,018 --> 00:30:21,996
as well as on the
bottoms of his shoes.

590
00:30:22,020 --> 00:30:26,000
In other words, the defendant
was in Howard Wright's garage

591
00:30:26,024 --> 00:30:27,568
the night of the
murder, wasn't he?

592
00:30:27,592 --> 00:30:28,903
Objection.

593
00:30:28,927 --> 00:30:29,904
Calls for a conclusion.

594
00:30:29,928 --> 00:30:31,005
Withdraw the question.

595
00:30:31,029 --> 00:30:35,043
Uh... thank you,
Lieutenant Brooks.

596
00:30:35,067 --> 00:30:36,695
Uh, nothing further.

597
00:30:38,704 --> 00:30:40,715
Mr. Gaddis?

598
00:30:40,739 --> 00:30:41,749
No questions.

599
00:30:41,773 --> 00:30:43,985
Witness may step down.

600
00:30:44,009 --> 00:30:46,888
This court is in recess
until 9:00 a.m. tomorrow.

601
00:30:51,316 --> 00:30:54,295
You lied to me, Tom.

602
00:30:54,319 --> 00:30:57,332
Chester, I didn't kill him.

603
00:30:57,356 --> 00:30:58,800
You were there!

604
00:30:58,824 --> 00:31:00,301
And the jury knows
you were there.

605
00:31:00,325 --> 00:31:01,669
I was there.

606
00:31:01,693 --> 00:31:03,171
But I swear, I didn't kill him!

607
00:31:03,195 --> 00:31:05,073
The whole thrust of my case

608
00:31:05,097 --> 00:31:08,576
was proving that
you weren't there.

609
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:12,747
I discredited the eyewitnesses,
I discredited the police.

610
00:31:12,771 --> 00:31:16,184
And now I look like a fool.

611
00:31:16,208 --> 00:31:20,009
And you, Tom... you
look like you're guilty.

612
00:31:22,948 --> 00:31:24,940
What do we do now?

613
00:31:27,285 --> 00:31:29,516
I'm putting you on the stand.

614
00:31:31,556 --> 00:31:33,267
Is that a good idea?

615
00:31:33,291 --> 00:31:36,437
It's the only idea.

616
00:31:36,461 --> 00:31:38,606
I don't want to put
any pressure on you,

617
00:31:38,630 --> 00:31:42,176
but this better be the greatest
performance of your life.

618
00:31:42,200 --> 00:31:43,778
Because if it's not,

619
00:31:43,802 --> 00:31:46,101
you'll spend the rest
of your life in prison.

620
00:31:59,518 --> 00:32:01,987
Everything David
Bennett said is true.

621
00:32:04,022 --> 00:32:07,301
I conspired with him
and Judge Levin to fix

622
00:32:07,325 --> 00:32:10,405
the outcome of certain cases.

623
00:32:10,429 --> 00:32:13,374
And I paid Howard Wright to
lose a case against someone

624
00:32:13,398 --> 00:32:16,277
who was instrumental
in my winning

625
00:32:16,301 --> 00:32:19,647
a multimillion-dollar lawsuit.

626
00:32:19,671 --> 00:32:21,333
But I did not kill him.

627
00:32:24,075 --> 00:32:27,855
What were you doing in
his workshop that night?

628
00:32:27,879 --> 00:32:29,957
Well, he was afraid the
police were onto him,

629
00:32:29,981 --> 00:32:34,214
so he asked me for
$100,000 to leave town.

630
00:32:36,254 --> 00:32:38,132
So I got the money, and I, uh,

631
00:32:38,156 --> 00:32:42,937
I stopped by at 8:00 to
deliver it like we'd arranged.

632
00:32:42,961 --> 00:32:46,140
Only, when I got
there, he was dead.

633
00:32:46,164 --> 00:32:49,157
Somebody else had shot him?

634
00:32:50,635 --> 00:32:53,848
I swear, under oath,

635
00:32:53,872 --> 00:32:56,933
that I did not kill
Howard Wright.

636
00:32:57,976 --> 00:32:59,487
Thank you.

637
00:32:59,511 --> 00:33:01,742
Mr. Matlock?

638
00:33:05,450 --> 00:33:11,165
So... so you paid Howard Wright

639
00:33:11,189 --> 00:33:13,734
to lose the evidence

640
00:33:13,758 --> 00:33:16,771
in a case against
Jimmy Giles, is that right?

641
00:33:16,795 --> 00:33:18,973
I already said that, yes.

642
00:33:18,997 --> 00:33:22,877
Well, you had a... you had a
very good reason to kill him.

643
00:33:22,901 --> 00:33:25,513
I mean, if he had
gone to the police,

644
00:33:25,537 --> 00:33:28,549
why... you would've
been ruined, wouldn't you?

645
00:33:28,573 --> 00:33:29,917
Yes.

646
00:33:29,941 --> 00:33:31,953
So you were paying him off.

647
00:33:31,977 --> 00:33:33,421
Uh, what would...

648
00:33:33,445 --> 00:33:36,390
what would keep him from asking

649
00:33:36,414 --> 00:33:38,974
for another hundred thousand?

650
00:33:40,285 --> 00:33:41,629
Nothing.

651
00:33:41,653 --> 00:33:44,398
So you-you couldn't be sure.

652
00:33:44,422 --> 00:33:47,001
I mean, you couldn't be
sure he wouldn't ask for more,

653
00:33:47,025 --> 00:33:49,136
you couldn't be sure he
wouldn't go to the police,

654
00:33:49,160 --> 00:33:51,506
so you did the only thing
you could be sure of...

655
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:53,741
You went to his house
that night, didn't you?

656
00:33:53,765 --> 00:33:55,743
- I went to bring him the mon...
- Yes or no?

657
00:33:55,767 --> 00:33:57,011
Yes, I went to his house.

658
00:33:57,035 --> 00:33:58,613
- And you killed him.
- No.

659
00:33:58,637 --> 00:34:01,616
He was dead when
I got there, I swear.

660
00:34:01,640 --> 00:34:04,819
So, just-just like
your counselor

661
00:34:04,843 --> 00:34:07,522
said to my witness
yesterday, we're just

662
00:34:07,546 --> 00:34:10,491
- supposed to take your word for it.
- Yes.

663
00:34:10,515 --> 00:34:12,916
I left the money there.

664
00:34:13,952 --> 00:34:15,429
You what?

665
00:34:15,453 --> 00:34:17,498
I was so frightened
when I saw him

666
00:34:17,522 --> 00:34:20,167
that I ran away
without the money.

667
00:34:20,191 --> 00:34:23,437
You left $100,000?

668
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:26,474
The first thing I saw when I
went in was him on the floor.

669
00:34:26,498 --> 00:34:30,244
I thought he'd slipped in
the varnish and hit his head.

670
00:34:30,268 --> 00:34:33,080
I put my briefcase down
and went over to him.

671
00:34:33,104 --> 00:34:35,883
But then I saw the blood.

672
00:34:35,907 --> 00:34:37,985
All of a sudden, the, uh,

673
00:34:38,009 --> 00:34:40,922
the alarm on his watch went off.

674
00:34:40,946 --> 00:34:43,991
I ran out of there
as fast as I could.

675
00:34:44,015 --> 00:34:46,746
I didn't even remember the
money till the next morning.

676
00:34:48,119 --> 00:34:50,765
There was no briefcase found,

677
00:34:50,789 --> 00:34:53,334
let alone $100,000.

678
00:34:53,358 --> 00:34:55,770
Well, that may be.

679
00:34:55,794 --> 00:34:58,059
But I left it there.

680
00:35:02,233 --> 00:35:04,259
Mr. Matlock?

681
00:35:05,303 --> 00:35:06,965
Are you finished?

682
00:35:08,306 --> 00:35:11,419
Y-Your Honor, um...

683
00:35:11,443 --> 00:35:15,723
I'd like to ask for a recess
till tomorrow morning.

684
00:35:15,747 --> 00:35:18,960
We just had a
recess, Mr. Matlock.

685
00:35:18,984 --> 00:35:21,662
Do you have any idea how
backlogged this court is?

686
00:35:21,686 --> 00:35:24,332
It-It's personal, Your Honor.

687
00:35:24,356 --> 00:35:26,734
I don't... I don't feel so good.

688
00:35:26,758 --> 00:35:29,737
It must be something
I ate for breakfast.

689
00:35:29,761 --> 00:35:33,741
This court is in recess
until 9:30 a.m. tomorrow.

690
00:35:39,904 --> 00:35:41,736
You all right?

691
00:35:43,475 --> 00:35:44,785
I'm fine.

692
00:35:44,809 --> 00:35:48,556
I just had to make sure
he gave me the recess.

693
00:35:48,580 --> 00:35:51,559
But you had that guy...
Why did you stop?

694
00:35:51,583 --> 00:35:53,694
'Cause he's telling the truth.

695
00:35:53,718 --> 00:35:54,929
The truth?

696
00:35:54,953 --> 00:35:57,431
Oh, Ben, that story
about the money is crazy.

697
00:35:57,455 --> 00:36:00,857
That's why he's got
to be telling the truth.

698
00:36:10,435 --> 00:36:13,247
I don't remember
seeing a briefcase.

699
00:36:13,271 --> 00:36:15,016
What did you do
after you walked in

700
00:36:15,040 --> 00:36:17,051
and found your husband?

701
00:36:17,075 --> 00:36:20,721
I ran into the
house, called 911,

702
00:36:20,745 --> 00:36:23,758
and I stayed in the house
until the police came.

703
00:36:23,782 --> 00:36:27,628
And you don't remember
seeing or picking up a briefcase?

704
00:36:27,652 --> 00:36:31,098
All I remember is
seeing my husband...

705
00:36:31,122 --> 00:36:33,567
lying there, dead.

706
00:36:33,591 --> 00:36:35,936
I'm sorry.

707
00:36:35,960 --> 00:36:37,872
I got it.

708
00:36:37,896 --> 00:36:40,388
I got the money right here.

709
00:36:41,566 --> 00:36:42,877
Oh.

710
00:36:48,139 --> 00:36:49,116
Hello?

711
00:36:49,140 --> 00:36:51,619
Diana, dear, it-it's
your Aunt Sam.

712
00:36:51,643 --> 00:36:54,088
I just got a call from a
man who said he was

713
00:36:54,112 --> 00:36:55,790
a special prosecutor
or something

714
00:36:55,814 --> 00:36:57,324
out-out there in Atlanta.

715
00:36:57,348 --> 00:36:58,592
Ben Matlock?

716
00:36:58,616 --> 00:37:00,227
Yes, Ben Matlock.

717
00:37:00,251 --> 00:37:03,431
Anyway, he asked
me about the $100,000

718
00:37:03,455 --> 00:37:06,367
you just, uh, wired to
your account down here.

719
00:37:06,391 --> 00:37:09,470
I mean, you know, <i>our</i> account.

720
00:37:09,494 --> 00:37:11,038
What'd he want to know?

721
00:37:11,062 --> 00:37:13,040
Where the money came from.

722
00:37:13,064 --> 00:37:14,408
What'd you tell him?

723
00:37:14,432 --> 00:37:16,610
That I didn't know.

724
00:37:16,634 --> 00:37:18,579
That's all I <i>could</i> tell him.

725
00:37:18,603 --> 00:37:23,234
Uh, dear, is-is-is
everything all right with you?

726
00:37:24,442 --> 00:37:26,120
Everything's fine, Aunt Sam.

727
00:37:26,144 --> 00:37:27,421
It's nothing.

728
00:37:27,445 --> 00:37:29,290
But thank you for calling.

729
00:37:29,314 --> 00:37:33,227
And if he calls you again,
you let me know, okay?

730
00:37:33,251 --> 00:37:34,962
All right, dear.

731
00:37:34,986 --> 00:37:36,330
Good-bye, dear.

732
00:37:36,354 --> 00:37:37,932
Bye.

733
00:37:46,898 --> 00:37:49,543
I'm supposed to hand-deliver
some documents to Frank Scanzie.

734
00:37:49,567 --> 00:37:50,978
Where's his office?

735
00:37:51,002 --> 00:37:52,580
Third door on the right.

736
00:38:23,735 --> 00:38:25,727
♪♪

737
00:38:45,089 --> 00:38:46,921
Be seated.

738
00:38:48,726 --> 00:38:50,738
Well...

739
00:38:50,762 --> 00:38:53,440
did breakfast agree with
you this morning, Mr. Matlock?

740
00:38:53,464 --> 00:38:56,744
Oh, very well, Your
Honor, very well.

741
00:38:56,768 --> 00:38:57,978
Good.

742
00:38:58,002 --> 00:39:00,381
Mr. Gaddis, next witness.

743
00:39:00,405 --> 00:39:02,850
Uh, Your Honor,

744
00:39:02,874 --> 00:39:05,119
the state would like
to reopen its case

745
00:39:05,143 --> 00:39:08,322
in order to present
an additional witness.

746
00:39:08,346 --> 00:39:11,025
I've already spoken
to Mr. Gaddis.

747
00:39:11,049 --> 00:39:12,459
Mr. Gaddis?

748
00:39:12,483 --> 00:39:15,663
I have no objections,
Your Honor.

749
00:39:15,687 --> 00:39:18,065
Very well. Call your witness.

750
00:39:18,089 --> 00:39:21,335
The state calls
Judge Diana Levin.

751
00:39:21,359 --> 00:39:23,771
Approximately how much money

752
00:39:23,795 --> 00:39:27,074
did this conspiracy
between you and Tom O'Hare

753
00:39:27,098 --> 00:39:30,144
and Judge Bennett
net you over the years?

754
00:39:30,168 --> 00:39:32,012
What conspiracy is that?

755
00:39:32,036 --> 00:39:34,248
The same conspiracy
that Tom O'Hare

756
00:39:34,272 --> 00:39:36,617
and Judge Bennett
admitted to in this courtroom.

757
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:38,385
I don't know anything about it.

758
00:39:38,409 --> 00:39:40,120
Mm-hmm, okay.

759
00:39:40,144 --> 00:39:42,256
You have a...

760
00:39:42,280 --> 00:39:45,092
an aunt named, uh,
Samantha Bergstrom

761
00:39:45,116 --> 00:39:47,161
down in Savannah, don't you?

762
00:39:47,185 --> 00:39:49,363
Yes, I do.

763
00:39:49,387 --> 00:39:50,731
And, uh...

764
00:39:50,755 --> 00:39:54,869
there are deposits that you
make into her bank account

765
00:39:54,893 --> 00:39:57,738
and she does
not, is that correct?

766
00:39:57,762 --> 00:39:59,340
My aunt is elderly.

767
00:39:59,364 --> 00:40:01,575
I worry about her,
so I send money

768
00:40:01,599 --> 00:40:03,878
that I earn as a
consultant to her bank

769
00:40:03,902 --> 00:40:05,212
in case of emergency.

770
00:40:05,236 --> 00:40:07,330
Come on, now, come on.

771
00:40:08,373 --> 00:40:10,784
Sam Bergstrom.

772
00:40:10,808 --> 00:40:13,454
$125,000.

773
00:40:13,478 --> 00:40:15,789
Sam Bergstrom.

774
00:40:15,813 --> 00:40:18,259
$110,000.

775
00:40:18,283 --> 00:40:20,294
Sam Bergstrom.

776
00:40:20,318 --> 00:40:23,254
$95,000.

777
00:40:24,455 --> 00:40:27,101
That's exactly the
same amount of money

778
00:40:27,125 --> 00:40:30,571
that Tom O'Hare
and David Bennett got,

779
00:40:30,595 --> 00:40:33,107
and they admitted
in this courtroom

780
00:40:33,131 --> 00:40:34,808
that they were payoffs.

781
00:40:34,832 --> 00:40:37,378
Not consultant fees, payoffs.

782
00:40:37,402 --> 00:40:40,948
I can only speak for
myself, Mr. Matlock.

783
00:40:40,972 --> 00:40:43,984
That money was for
services I rendered

784
00:40:44,008 --> 00:40:46,944
as a legal consultant.

785
00:40:48,146 --> 00:40:51,959
I subpoenaed the records of...

786
00:40:51,983 --> 00:40:55,462
of your aunt's bank account,

787
00:40:55,486 --> 00:40:58,799
and all the deposits
that were made

788
00:40:58,823 --> 00:41:01,802
match <i>exactly</i> the checks

789
00:41:01,826 --> 00:41:04,071
the Connor Corporation
made out to her.

790
00:41:04,095 --> 00:41:06,140
All except this...
this last one.

791
00:41:06,164 --> 00:41:07,441
This last one here.

792
00:41:07,465 --> 00:41:09,476
$100,000.

793
00:41:09,500 --> 00:41:13,213
Deposited the day after
Howard Wright was murdered.

794
00:41:13,237 --> 00:41:15,263
Where'd that come from?

795
00:41:16,874 --> 00:41:20,454
That was money I was
keeping in a safe-deposit box.

796
00:41:20,478 --> 00:41:24,491
Are you sure it wasn't
the money Tom O'Hare left

797
00:41:24,515 --> 00:41:26,827
in Howard Wright's workshop?

798
00:41:26,851 --> 00:41:27,962
I'm positive.

799
00:41:27,986 --> 00:41:29,563
Mm-hmm. What if Howard Wright

800
00:41:29,587 --> 00:41:32,566
had told the police
a-about this conspiracy

801
00:41:32,590 --> 00:41:34,802
that Tom O'Hare roped him into?

802
00:41:34,826 --> 00:41:37,338
That would've been the end
of your career, too, wouldn't it?

803
00:41:37,362 --> 00:41:41,041
Again, I don't know
anything about a conspiracy.

804
00:41:41,065 --> 00:41:42,042
Uh-huh.

805
00:41:42,066 --> 00:41:44,745
Uh-huh.

806
00:41:44,769 --> 00:41:47,047
Did, uh...

807
00:41:47,071 --> 00:41:49,817
did Tom O'Hare
tell you he was going

808
00:41:49,841 --> 00:41:53,120
over to Howard Wright's
house at 8:00 that night?

809
00:41:53,144 --> 00:41:54,521
No.

810
00:41:54,545 --> 00:41:56,790
He told <i>me</i> he did.

811
00:41:56,814 --> 00:41:58,092
Must have lied.

812
00:41:58,116 --> 00:41:59,893
You sure you didn't
get there ahead of him

813
00:41:59,917 --> 00:42:01,328
and shoot Howard
Wright to death?

814
00:42:01,352 --> 00:42:02,629
No!

815
00:42:02,653 --> 00:42:05,499
And then when Tom O'Hare
arrived ahead of schedule,

816
00:42:05,523 --> 00:42:07,501
you hid so he wouldn't see you,

817
00:42:07,525 --> 00:42:08,936
- didn't you?
- No.

818
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:11,438
And then, after he
went tearing out of there,

819
00:42:11,462 --> 00:42:15,576
you thought, "Well, why waste
all this money on a dead man?"

820
00:42:15,600 --> 00:42:17,344
So you took it

821
00:42:17,368 --> 00:42:20,047
and deposited it in your
aunt's account, didn't you?

822
00:42:20,071 --> 00:42:21,181
No.

823
00:42:21,205 --> 00:42:23,350
Bet you wish you
hadn't done that.

824
00:42:23,374 --> 00:42:25,285
That's what put us onto you.

825
00:42:25,309 --> 00:42:26,854
Mistake.

826
00:42:26,878 --> 00:42:29,423
She should've kept that cash
and bought some new shoes

827
00:42:29,447 --> 00:42:31,558
or a robe or something.

828
00:42:31,582 --> 00:42:33,327
May I see your watch?

829
00:42:35,319 --> 00:42:40,167
Oh... it's got one of those
alarms that goes off every hour.

830
00:42:40,191 --> 00:42:41,402
Another mistake.

831
00:42:41,426 --> 00:42:43,003
You should've left this at home.

832
00:42:43,027 --> 00:42:44,271
Howard Wright didn't have

833
00:42:44,295 --> 00:42:46,940
a watch like this;
neither did Tom O'Hare.

834
00:42:46,964 --> 00:42:49,810
It was your watch that
went off that night, wasn't it?

835
00:42:49,834 --> 00:42:51,945
Thousands of people
have a watch like this.

836
00:42:51,969 --> 00:42:53,301
That doesn't prove a thing.

837
00:42:54,839 --> 00:42:58,886
But now... the murder weapon.

838
00:42:58,910 --> 00:43:00,587
Mm!

839
00:43:00,611 --> 00:43:03,877
There would be proof,
there would be proof.

840
00:43:05,183 --> 00:43:08,095
The gun that killed
Howard Wright

841
00:43:08,119 --> 00:43:10,931
and a law clerk
named Terry Lasher...

842
00:43:10,955 --> 00:43:13,267
The riflings on...

843
00:43:13,291 --> 00:43:16,703
both bullets in both
murders match exactly.

844
00:43:16,727 --> 00:43:18,338
Unfortunately...

845
00:43:18,362 --> 00:43:21,208
the gun has never been found.

846
00:43:21,232 --> 00:43:25,879
That is... until yesterday.

847
00:43:25,903 --> 00:43:28,682
The police found it

848
00:43:28,706 --> 00:43:31,452
in a desk drawer in
the defendant's office.

849
00:43:31,476 --> 00:43:34,121
Then why on earth am <i>I</i> up here?

850
00:43:34,145 --> 00:43:36,580
Because you put it there.

851
00:43:40,785 --> 00:43:42,963
I called...

852
00:43:42,987 --> 00:43:46,433
your aunt down in
Savannah about that...

853
00:43:46,457 --> 00:43:48,335
$100,000.

854
00:43:48,359 --> 00:43:50,737
She called you, didn't she?

855
00:43:50,761 --> 00:43:52,439
And then...

856
00:43:52,463 --> 00:43:54,408
you did just what
I figured you'd do

857
00:43:54,432 --> 00:43:57,778
when you figured I
was getting too close.

858
00:43:57,802 --> 00:44:00,414
You killed two
birds with one stone.

859
00:44:00,438 --> 00:44:02,416
You got rid of the
murder weapon,

860
00:44:02,440 --> 00:44:05,152
and you did it in such a
way as to strengthen the case

861
00:44:05,176 --> 00:44:06,620
against the
defendant, didn't you?

862
00:44:06,644 --> 00:44:09,204
No.

863
00:44:14,552 --> 00:44:17,920
You ever see yourself
on TV, Judge Levin?

864
00:44:19,457 --> 00:44:23,904
We had TV cameras hidden
all over Mr. O'Hare's office.

865
00:44:23,928 --> 00:44:27,074
But this is my favorite
angle, right here.

866
00:44:27,098 --> 00:44:30,244
It shows his desk real clearly.

867
00:44:31,269 --> 00:44:33,213
And there you are.

868
00:44:33,237 --> 00:44:35,229
See? Isn't that you?

869
00:44:36,707 --> 00:44:41,577
Hiding that unregistered
gun in his desk.

870
00:44:43,080 --> 00:44:46,493
The gun that killed Howard
Wright and Terry Lasher.

871
00:44:48,186 --> 00:44:50,178
Why?

872
00:44:52,823 --> 00:44:57,070
So no one would
ever know the person...

873
00:44:57,094 --> 00:45:00,223
who really killed them...

874
00:45:02,200 --> 00:45:04,032
was you.

875
00:45:13,878 --> 00:45:15,689
Very nice, Mr. Matlock.

876
00:45:15,713 --> 00:45:17,875
Well, back at you.

877
00:45:19,817 --> 00:45:22,329
Mr. O'Hare has
asked me to inquire

878
00:45:22,353 --> 00:45:25,599
whether you would represent
him in his forthcoming trial

879
00:45:25,623 --> 00:45:27,167
for obstruction of justice,

880
00:45:27,191 --> 00:45:31,004
fraud and about 12
other felony counts.

881
00:45:31,028 --> 00:45:33,507
Uh, why don't you do it?

882
00:45:33,531 --> 00:45:36,558
I've had about all I
can take of Mr. O'Hare.

883
00:45:37,602 --> 00:45:39,434
Me, too.

884
00:45:44,008 --> 00:45:46,353
- Nice suit.
- Mm, yeah, yeah.

885
00:45:46,377 --> 00:45:47,754
Nice tie.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
